
find /home/user -name "myfile.txt"    --> Find a file by name (case-sensitive)


find / -iname "myfile.txt"   -->  Find a file by name (case-insensitive)


find . -type f -name "*.log"  --> Only find regular files (not directories)
find /home -type f -empty     --> to find empty files


find . -type d -name "*log"   --> to find directory
find /home -type d -empty     --> to find empty directories


find . -type l -name "*log"   --> to find Symbolic/Soft link
readlink -f /var/lock            --> to know physical path of symbolic link
find / -type l -exec readlink -f {} \; 2>/dev/null | sort | uniq -c | awk '$1 > 2'    --> to list files which has more than 2 symbolic links, 
                                                                                          readlink -f = resolve it to its final absolute path


find . -type f -links 2 -exec ls -li {} \; --> finding hard-linked regular files, not symbolic links.
find / -xdev -inum 123456 -ls 2>/dev/null   --> it will provide in same file system, -xdev prevents find to cross the filesystem
find / -inum 123456 -ls 2>/dev/null  --> Find all pathnames having inode number 123456, including matches from different filesystems.



find . -user viswa 2>/dev/null   --> to search based on user
find / -not -user viswa -type f 2>/dev/null
find / -uid 1001 -type f 2>/dev/null      --> to serach using user id

find /home -user viswa -type f -ls 2>/dev/null  --> to show details of files owned by viswa


find . -group flm 2>/dev/null     --> to search based on group
find / -not -group flm -type f 2>/dev/null
find / -gid 1002 -type f 2>/dev/null      --> to serach using group id


find /home -group flm -type f -ls 2>/dev/null  --> to show details of files owned by group


find . -perm 777    --> to search based on permissions


find / -type f -size +100M   --> Find files by size more than 100M (e.g., larger than 100 MB)
find / -type f -size -100M   --> Find files by size less than 100M 
find / -type f -size  100M   --> Find files with exactly 100M 
c → bytes
k → KiB
M → MiB
G → GiB


find /path/to/search -type f -mtime 0     --> Modified within the last 24 hours
find /path/to/search -type f -mtime -7    --> files modified within last 7 days
find /path/to/search -type f -mtime +7    --> Files modified more than 7 days ago


find /path/to/search -type f -mmin -30    --> files modified within last 30 min
find /path/to/search -type f -mmin +30    --> Files modified more than 30 min ago


find /home -type f -atime 0  --> files accessed with in 24 hrs
find /home -type f -atime -7  --> files accessed with in last 7 days
find /home -type f -atime +7  --> files accessed more than 7 days ago


find /home -type f -amin 30  --> files accessed with in 30 min
find /home -type f -atime +30 --> files accessed more than 30 min ago


find /home -type f -ctime 0     --> Finds files whose inode metadata/status changed within approximately the last 24 hours
For example, changing: permissions , ownership, link count, file contents can update ctime.
find /home -type f -ctime -7    --> within 7 days
find /home -type f -ctime +7    --> more than 7 days


find /home -type f -cmin -30  --> within 30 min
find /home -type f -cmin -30  --> more than 30 min ago


find /home/viswa -type f \( -name "*.log" -o -name "*.txt" \)   -->  or condition


find /home/viswa -type f ! -name "*.log"      --> not condition
find /home/viswa -type f -not -name "*.log"


find /home -type f -print0 | xargs -0 -r ls -l  --> This safely handles filenames such as: my file.txt, report 2026.txt
xargs ls -l  --> effectively builds something like: ls -l /home/viswa/a.txt /home/viswa/b.txt /home/viswa/c.txt
-print0 , xargs -0     --> Read NULL-separated filenames safely.
-r --> Don't run the command if there is no input.


find . -type f -exec grep -l "search_string" {} \;   --> Combine find with grep to search file contents , -l -> Only print the filename, not the matching line.
                          -Hn                        --> -H    → print filename , -n    → print line number
                                                     --> {} represents each file found by find
                                                     --> \; Marks the end of the -exec command

explanation:
============
find .
   ↓
Find everything under current directory
   ↓
-type f
   ↓
Keep only regular files
   ↓
-exec
   ↓
Run grep on each file
   ↓
grep -l "viswa"   --> -l Only print the filename, not the matching line.
     -Hn          --> -H    → print filename , -n    → print line number
   ↓
Print only files containing "viswa"
